8
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:57,391
Today we will talk to Eric about the world of observability technology. Welcome to the show Eric.

9
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:59,070
It's great to have you here.

10
00:00:59,070 --> 00:01:00,911
It's great to be here. Thanks for having me.

11
00:01:00,911 --> 00:01:04,150
Of course.

12
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:09,590
So, for listeners who may be less familiar with the subject, how would you define observability?

13
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:11,750
Observability, that's a great question.

14
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:18,870
I mean, you can start with just the English word observe and that's looking at things.

15
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:26,710
But really as the internet technology has evolved, as more services have been given to people through servers and technology,

16
00:01:26,710 --> 00:01:32,870
there's become a need to understand how the systems are operating,

17
00:01:32,870 --> 00:01:43,190
understand what's going on inside of them and really be able to use that to debug what happens when things go wrong because things will inevitably go wrong, right?

18
00:01:43,190 --> 00:01:50,470
And that notion of being able to observe, understand what's going on in the system and then actually take action on the system as a whole,

19
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:55,310
I would bundle up all together into observability.

20
00:01:55,310 --> 00:02:02,310
So, I think of specifically observability in terms of logs and metrics and traces.

21
00:02:02,310 --> 00:02:04,310
Is that all or am I not covering something?

22
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:06,350
Oh yeah, absolutely.

23
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:16,470
Like when you take it down to fundamental data level, the tools that people are using, the data that people are using to observe is logs,

24
00:02:16,470 --> 00:02:24,510
all those things we write in our code that get dumped out to see, metrics which give you a much broader view, you can aggregate them,

25
00:02:24,510 --> 00:02:31,350
you can see across a much wider view of what's going on before you actually like drill down into an issue,

26
00:02:31,350 --> 00:02:38,430
and traces of course where especially with the advent and expansion of microservices and service orientation,

27
00:02:38,430 --> 00:02:43,830
being able to trace a request as it's running through your infrastructures very important.

28
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:55,110
The one thing I would add to it is the alerting infrastructure because there's definitely a need to respond to this data, alert and bring people in.

29
00:02:55,110 --> 00:03:03,470
And then I think most recently also agentic and AI and all of that is coming into the picture and that's one of those things that

30
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:09,471
I feel like the industry as a whole is figuring out where it fits and how it fits. Absolutely.

31
00:03:09,471 --> 00:03:14,470
So, right now we see a lot of shift toward a microservices based framework.

32
00:03:14,470 --> 00:03:22,271
Would you say that observability is becoming increasingly critical in systems like that and how so? Yes, absolutely.

33
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:26,950
There's a fun meme about how to draw an owl.

34
00:03:26,950 --> 00:03:37,350
I'm not sure how many of your listeners are familiar with it, but it starts with a picture of two circles and then it goes to draw the effing owl and it's like a fully fleshed out owl with all the details and all of that.

35
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:49,110
And some people liken I've seen that meme used to respond to Kubernetes where it's like, here's how to do something in Kubernetes, deploy hello world app, now deploy everything else.

36
00:03:49,110 --> 00:03:59,950
And it's a big jump, but with microservices, with things like Kubernetes, there's a lot more kind of independent moving pieces and there's good reason to have them.

37
00:03:59,950 --> 00:04:08,910
It separates concerns, it gives you independent access of scale so that you can adjust your system to align with what's actually happening.

38
00:04:08,910 --> 00:04:17,470
But along with that comes a complexity of knowing like, is it this part of the system or is it that part of the system or is it this service over here?

39
00:04:17,470 --> 00:04:26,150
And that's really requiring a lot more stuff out of the observability systems, out of what's going on and where are things happening.

40
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:37,670
So, it brings me perfectly into talking about the general idea here of tight coupling and decoupling observability frameworks.

41
00:04:37,670 --> 00:04:44,670
Of course, what you just mentioned is absolutely true, that meme of an owl, a lot of people can relate especially with their work, right?

42
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:54,151
But when you think of the observability stack at most places today, what does a typical tightly coupled framework look like? Can you describe it for me?

43
00:04:54,151 --> 00:05:03,430
Yeah, so the tightly coupled observability stacks really it comes back to people start with well, I want to be able to see this thing.

44
00:05:03,430 --> 00:05:08,710
I want to be able to see my logs or whatever and they're like, okay, well what's going to let me see the logs?

45
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:14,750
And then they get into what's the data, where am I going to store it and how am I going to interact with it and stuff.

46
00:05:14,750 --> 00:05:22,590
And so in the logging space, I feel like this notion of a tightly coupled just log stack, Splunk was the first one I ever interacted with.

47
00:05:22,590 --> 00:05:33,030
And this was many years ago where you just tail your logs, you throw them in Splunk and you write some things and you find stuff in the logs and it gives you an aggregated source of logs.

48
00:05:33,030 --> 00:05:41,070
So, you have logs from a whole bunch of different services, a whole bunch of different physical machines coming into one place so that you can see them.

49
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:44,390
And so, Splunk is a tightly coupled thing.

50
00:05:44,390 --> 00:05:51,790
It has a UI component that interacts with its database layer and there's forwarder agents that are used to push data in.

51
00:05:51,800 --> 00:06:04,350
Over time that's evolved like Elasticsearch has gone into that space and there's this thing called the ELK stack, the Elastic Logstash Kibana stack.

52
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:15,470
And so, Elastic, the open source project, you can use Logstash to grab your logs, you can push them into Elastic, you can then use Kibana on top of that in order to see the logs and interact with it.

53
00:06:15,470 --> 00:06:20,950
Even in the Grafana world, Grafana started as a UI for interacting with metrics.

54
00:06:20,950 --> 00:06:25,630
But they've also started building in logging capabilities.

55
00:06:25,630 --> 00:06:33,790
They have this open source thing called Loki DB which is a database for logs and they've leaned in heavily to this thing called OpenTelemetry which

56
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:40,350
is an open source kind of agent tree for collecting logs, metrics and traces.

57
00:06:40,350 --> 00:06:49,590
But I've mentioned Splunk, I've mentioned the ELK stack, I've mentioned Grafana and even like the ELK stack and Grafana, they're open.

58
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:54,830
They're open source, they're wide open, but you always use them in a stack.

59
00:06:54,830 --> 00:06:56,710
Each of these things is always vertically integrated.

60
00:06:56,710 --> 00:07:02,510
You've got your UI on top of your database on top of your data acquisition.

61
00:07:02,510 --> 00:07:10,070
And even if you go to the cloud vendors, you go to Datadog, Datadog has a way you get data and then they vertically integrate everything in their nice walled gardens.

62
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:13,150
You go to Dynatrace, it's the same thing.

63
00:07:13,150 --> 00:07:16,790
You go to New Relic, it's the same thing.

64
00:07:16,790 --> 00:07:26,870
And really in this world, everyone has kind of said, well, we're going to get the data from somewhere, but then we're going to put it in our database and we're going to build our UI against our database and that's going to be a thing.

65
00:07:26,870 --> 00:07:33,711
And that's really the vertically oriented stack that kind of gets you stuck in a walled garden once you put your data in there. I see.

66
00:07:33,711 --> 00:07:37,310
We will talk more about some of these examples that you shared,

67
00:07:37,310 --> 00:07:48,030
but I want to talk more about the general organizational structure of these coupled stacks since they're stacked on top of each other, where do you think the pain shows up?

68
00:07:48,030 --> 00:07:50,390
Like what breaks first at an organizational scale?

69
00:07:50,390 --> 00:07:57,830
Can you share an example of where vendor lock-in pain that you may have witnessed because of this?

70
00:07:57,830 --> 00:08:05,630
Yeah, I mean, there's lots of examples of where vendor lock-in kind of comes in, but the most acute is I mean,

71
00:08:05,630 --> 00:08:11,830
if you start from the beginning and you start from nothing, it's pretty easy to believe, oh, I'll just grab this system, I'll put everything in there.

72
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:17,870
I'm going to have all my logs, all my traces, all my metrics in this one system.

73
00:08:17,870 --> 00:08:26,030
And as long as you only have one product and one small group of teams or one small group of people working on a product, you'll probably stick with that system because everyone has learned it,

74
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:31,510
they know how it works and they'll do their things.

75
00:08:31,510 --> 00:08:36,830
But let's say your company grows, it expands, some other team comes in.

76
00:08:36,830 --> 00:08:39,390
That other team might be like, no, we like this system.

77
00:08:39,390 --> 00:08:49,190
We want to use this and we're going to use Loki DB with Grafana and OpenTelemetry and another team is like, we're going to use Logstash with Kibana and Elastic.

78
00:08:49,190 --> 00:08:51,750
And with the introduction of microservices,

79
00:08:51,760 --> 00:09:04,710
you tend to have organizations where a team owns a service and that team is supposed to kind of have some sort of autonomy about how that service works.

80
00:09:04,710 --> 00:09:11,790
And a lot of times that leads into these other knock-on infrastructure choices of where do the logs go and these other things.

81
00:09:11,790 --> 00:09:20,270
And so, at especially larger enterprises, I feel like there's generally a constant struggle for a new team,

82
00:09:20,270 --> 00:09:27,630
a new product line coming up wanting to be agile and quick and adopt some brand new thing while

83
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:36,830
there being some standard that there's some body of ivory tower type people in the enterprise saying, everybody must use this thing because that consolidates all the things.

84
00:09:36,830 --> 00:09:40,630
And then at especially large organizations, there might be some acquisitions.

85
00:09:40,630 --> 00:09:42,630
They might buy a company.

86
00:09:42,630 --> 00:09:45,950
That company knew nothing about the ivory tower in your enterprise.

87
00:09:45,950 --> 00:09:47,670
That company knew nothing about your new product.

88
00:09:47,670 --> 00:09:50,430
They just made decisions all on their own.

89
00:09:50,430 --> 00:09:59,350
And now you end up with this sprawl where each independent vertically or integrated stack has some data in it.

90
00:09:59,350 --> 00:10:04,070
But when you want to know what's going on across all of them, it's hard.

91
00:10:04,070 --> 00:10:06,191
You can't figure it out. You can't really look across them.

92
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:09,790
You're switching from one tool to the other to the other.

93
00:10:09,790 --> 00:10:14,590
And then you've got to take on these massive Okay, we acquired you.

94
00:10:14,590 --> 00:10:19,790
Now you need to move all of your log data into this system so that we can actually bundle it into the things that we're doing.

95
00:10:19,790 --> 00:10:22,150
And you get into big movements like that.

96
00:10:22,150 --> 00:10:31,790
You get into political battles with that brand new product team who's like they're in charge of doing this brand new thing that everybody loves,

97
00:10:31,790 --> 00:10:38,070
but they're doing it on some new infrastructure that's now causing a bunch of consternation for other people and political battles.

98
00:10:38,070 --> 00:10:43,830
And that's one of the places where it really really shows up as a strong pain point.

99
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:49,030
So, we will talk more about the solution here being decoupling.

100
00:10:49,030 --> 00:10:52,830
But I want to talk a little about the reasoning behind this.

101
00:10:52,830 --> 00:11:06,031
It seems like from my understanding the business intelligence world is decoupled into these a three-layer cake, if you think of visualization, data infrastructure, and then ETL. Yep.

102
00:11:06,031 --> 00:11:11,470
Why hasn't the observability world followed the same path till now?

103
00:11:11,470 --> 00:11:14,110
So, it's not actually a why hasn't it followed it.

104
00:11:14,110 --> 00:11:16,390
It's just a question of timeline.

105
00:11:16,390 --> 00:11:20,870
And this is also something that we've noticed as a company at Imply.

106
00:11:20,870 --> 00:11:26,630
We've had a number of customers in the business intelligence world and a number of customers in the observability world.

107
00:11:26,630 --> 00:11:36,150
And really what I think it is is that it's just the business intelligence market has had more time to evolve than observability and security.

108
00:11:36,150 --> 00:11:41,390
Observability and security are business intelligence 20-30 years ago.

109
00:11:41,390 --> 00:11:47,950
And if we go back to where business intelligence was, you're exactly right to say that there's a three-layer cake today.

110
00:11:47,950 --> 00:11:50,710
But you go back to where it started with decision control systems.

111
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:57,270
These are the really old systems. You might have heard of like Siebel systems and some other things like that.

112
00:11:57,270 --> 00:11:59,990
Or SAP kind of started back there.

113
00:11:59,990 --> 00:12:07,790
These are systems that came around that were starting to say, "Hey, we can collect some data to help business people make business decisions."

114
00:12:07,790 --> 00:12:10,070
And they were called decision control systems.

115
00:12:10,070 --> 00:12:13,750
And these started out as vertically integrated stacks.

116
00:12:13,750 --> 00:12:16,750
Because at the time the technology we didn't have databases.

117
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:20,030
We didn't have SQL at that time. We didn't have those things.

118
00:12:20,030 --> 00:12:22,030
It was well, someone wrote some code.

119
00:12:22,030 --> 00:12:26,350
They're storing some data and now they're making it visual.

120
00:12:26,350 --> 00:12:31,110
Then over evolution that decoupled into a reporting layer on top of databases.

121
00:12:31,110 --> 00:12:34,710
And that's when the relational database started coming into the world.

122
00:12:34,710 --> 00:12:36,750
That's when SQL really started taking hold.

123
00:12:36,750 --> 00:12:43,390
You had like Crystal Reports and a few things like that enter into the market and drive things.

124
00:12:43,390 --> 00:12:45,470
MicroStrategy is another company that came in as a visualization layer.

125
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:52,710
But there was this separation of the data layer from the visualization layer.

126
00:12:52,710 --> 00:12:55,670
And that drove a lot of innovation.

127
00:12:55,670 --> 00:13:03,830
And then people realized that oh, now we need another set of tools to help us get data into this data layer. And that gave birth to ETL.

128
00:13:03,830 --> 00:13:13,870
And really that came from the same thing that I was just mentioning earlier in terms of the pain of a walled garden, the pain of a vertically integrated stack.

129
00:13:13,870 --> 00:13:22,270
And the reason it evolved in business intelligence is that need to be able to take the data to a lot of different places.

130
00:13:22,270 --> 00:13:33,390
If when you acquire that company, you know their data is going to be accessible via SQL, it's so much easier to make sure that the way you're interacting with the data is going to be able to work with it.

131
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:44,150
It's so much easier to take those tools and maybe you do want to migrate them from this database to that database, but you're not whole hog changing everything soup to nuts.

132
00:13:44,150 --> 00:13:49,390
You're just changing one little piece, moving stuff, and keeping people's workflows intact.

133
00:13:49,390 --> 00:14:00,310
And so, the business intelligence market evolved very explicitly from tightly integrated verticalized walled gardens into this decoupled

134
00:14:00,310 --> 00:14:06,670
three-layer cake of the visualization on the data platform on the ETL and data acquisition.

135
00:14:06,670 --> 00:14:08,390
And I mean,

136
00:14:08,390 --> 00:14:18,949
that is exactly where observability and security are going as well because that is the natural evolution of a tightly coupled verticalized stack.

137
00:14:18,949 --> 00:14:20,870
So, that's very interesting.

138
00:14:20,870 --> 00:14:26,871
It makes me want to ask you more questions about this layered model and architecture of the decoupling. Mhm.

139
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:33,390
When we talk about decoupling the observability layer, what are the distinct layers that you see?

140
00:14:33,390 --> 00:14:35,670
What goes through the architecture?

141
00:14:35,670 --> 00:14:42,750
Is there an ingest layer, a data storage layer, query compute layer, visualization layer?

142
00:14:42,750 --> 00:14:44,870
Yes, I think it's the same layers in business intelligence.

143
00:14:44,870 --> 00:14:51,310
My general take on things is that history always repeats itself. There's so many different ways you can say it. Like there's nothing new under the sun.

144
00:14:51,320 --> 00:14:53,750
History always repeats itself.

145
00:14:53,750 --> 00:15:00,430
Like what we see is the exact same pattern repeating itself just with slight variations.

146
00:15:00,430 --> 00:15:05,069
And so, absolutely the business intelligence three-layer cake, that is how it's going to decouple.

147
00:15:05,069 --> 00:15:06,630
We already see it with OpenTelemetry.

148
00:15:06,640 --> 00:15:10,590
OpenTelemetry is basically the equivalent of the ETL layer.

149
00:15:10,590 --> 00:15:15,790
It's an open-source way to get data into a system.

150
00:15:15,790 --> 00:15:17,990
It can route to all sorts of different systems.

151
00:15:17,990 --> 00:15:20,431
You can run one OTEL collector. OTEL short for OpenTelemetry.

152
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:26,911
But you can run one OTEL collector and have it route to all the different places. Like that's already happening.

153
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:39,230
The thing that hasn't happened and is really what is I believe going to be happening or is happening is the separation of the data layer from the interaction layer and the UI.

154
00:15:39,230 --> 00:15:43,070
And how did that happen in the business intelligence world? SQL made it happen.

155
00:15:43,079 --> 00:15:47,710
There was basically SQL fundamentally became a de facto standard.

156
00:15:47,710 --> 00:15:51,630
There were all sorts of different ways of querying things back in the day, but SQL won.

157
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:54,030
Everything's on SQL and that's there.

158
00:15:54,040 --> 00:16:00,110
Now, why hasn't it worked quite that way in security and observability?

159
00:16:00,110 --> 00:16:04,630
It's because the data itself is slightly different.

160
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:17,350
And SQL is a very good language for when you have highly regimented structure to your data that you're interacting with and working with.

161
00:16:17,350 --> 00:16:22,790
And it's a great language for getting answers to questions about well-structured data.

162
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:32,590
What we find in the security and observability world is that people need something that looks and feels a bit more like programming.

163
00:16:32,590 --> 00:16:36,230
Less like just I'm writing a declarative statement and wanting to get something out.

164
00:16:36,230 --> 00:16:42,790
And more like programming where they want to be able to iterate, to change, to add things and flow.

165
00:16:42,790 --> 00:16:51,311
And so, really in the security and observability world, there's a whole new chunk of query languages. Splunk has SPL.

166
00:16:51,311 --> 00:16:56,470
Kibana uses its own flavor of things based on some JSON objects for querying things.

167
00:16:56,480 --> 00:17:02,790
Grafana and Loki DB use this thing called LogQL.

168
00:17:02,790 --> 00:17:11,871
In the Microsoft Azure world, Sentinel and there's this language called KQL, which was the Kusto query language. CrowdStrike has CQL.

169
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:16,189
There's a whole bunch of different query languages.

170
00:17:16,189 --> 00:17:21,310
And in the security and observability space, these query language, they're all pipe-based operators.

171
00:17:21,310 --> 00:17:28,950
So, they look a little bit more like your bash commands where you have a command, you pipe the results into another command, you pipe that into another command.

172
00:17:28,950 --> 00:17:41,750
And it allows for kind of more programming-style interactions that it turns out those are really useful and necessary in the observability and security world.

173
00:17:41,750 --> 00:17:44,430
And that's just logs.

174
00:17:44,430 --> 00:17:47,110
We get into metrics, there's PromQL for querying metrics.

175
00:17:47,110 --> 00:17:51,710
And that's a time series-oriented way to query metrics.

176
00:17:51,710 --> 00:17:56,710
It started with Prometheus and it's now pretty much a de facto standard, I would say.

177
00:17:56,710 --> 00:18:02,910
On the trace side of things, I think it feels like there's not one query language there.

178
00:18:02,910 --> 00:18:06,750
But really, so the answer to this decoupling is the query language.

179
00:18:06,750 --> 00:18:14,830
And the real thing that's different between observability and security and business intelligence is that there's not one query language.

180
00:18:14,830 --> 00:18:16,870
There's multiple different query languages.

181
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:22,150
But as a software engineer, as a developer,

182
00:18:22,150 --> 00:18:32,110
as someone working in data, a lot of people deep in like implementing databases will try to convince you that different query languages are not actually different.

183
00:18:32,110 --> 00:18:34,590
They all condense down to the same basic operations.

184
00:18:34,590 --> 00:18:36,830
It's just different ways of representing it.

185
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:41,390
And that I think is the key fundamental thing because that's actually true.

186
00:18:41,390 --> 00:18:44,669
But there's not many systems that have proven it out.

187
00:18:44,669 --> 00:18:49,510
There's not many systems that take multiple different query languages and execute all of them on the same data.

188
00:18:49,520 --> 00:18:55,031
I want to take an example here from the BI world. Uh-huh.

189
00:18:55,031 --> 00:18:57,390
Let's say I have Tableau or Grafana for visualization.

190
00:18:57,400 --> 00:18:59,909
Mhm.

191
00:18:59,909 --> 00:19:05,871
Snowflake or SQL Server for the data and Kafka or the ETL tools for acquisition. Yep.

192
00:19:05,871 --> 00:19:13,070
If this is my stack, similarly, how does that compare to this BI world separation?

193
00:19:13,070 --> 00:19:16,190
In practice, where do teams actually get stuck usually?

194
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:18,591
Okay. Yeah, so in practice.

195
00:19:18,591 --> 00:19:28,070
So, if we take Tableau on top of Snowflake or Databricks on top of DBT or Informatica, Fivetran, something like that.

196
00:19:28,070 --> 00:19:32,630
Your DBT Informatica, the ETL layer is your OpenTelemetry.

197
00:19:32,630 --> 00:19:35,470
That's pretty standard these days.

198
00:19:35,470 --> 00:19:40,190
The Tableau, if you're dealing with Splunk, Tableau would be Splunk UI.

199
00:19:40,190 --> 00:19:42,390
And then Snowflake would be your Splunk indexers.

200
00:19:42,390 --> 00:19:45,590
If you're in Elastic world, Tableau would be Kibana.

201
00:19:45,600 --> 00:19:51,710
And Snowflake would be the Elasticsearch cluster.

202
00:19:51,710 --> 00:19:58,990
If If in the Grafana world, Tableau would be Grafana and Snowflake would be Loki DB.

203
00:19:58,990 --> 00:20:04,990
If you're in DataDog land or Dynatrace land or New Relic land, you're just working with the UI.

204
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:13,670
So, all you see is the Tableau thing and then behind the scenes, each of those cloud vendors is running their own version of the Snowflake.

205
00:20:13,670 --> 00:20:21,430
And that's what they're seeing and those are the things where people get stuck is as long as they're staying in that walled garden, they're not that stuck.

206
00:20:21,430 --> 00:20:24,910
They're okay.

207
00:20:24,910 --> 00:20:28,151
The problem is it's hard to move walled gardens. It's hard to change.

208
00:20:28,151 --> 00:20:38,510
And so, as organizations expand, as different people bring new ideas, it's hard to evolve away from a specific walled garden.

209
00:20:38,510 --> 00:20:42,990
The other challenge with any of these walled gardens is when it's hard to move away from it,

210
00:20:42,990 --> 00:20:54,990
there's no economic incentive for the vendor who's delivering you the walled garden to maybe believe that you can pay more because it costs a bunch to actually move off.

211
00:20:54,990 --> 00:20:58,510
So, maybe you should give them a little bit more money cuz either way it's going to be more expensive to move.

212
00:20:58,520 --> 00:21:00,910
So, why not?

213
00:21:00,910 --> 00:21:05,830
And so, there ends up being an economic incentive or disincentive around it.

214
00:21:05,830 --> 00:21:17,350
And also, as an organization sprawls and expands, this is the real source of siloed data where you actually have data silos because they're stuck in vertically integrated stacks.

215
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:22,430
I want to hit on your first point, which is portability.

216
00:21:22,430 --> 00:21:33,590
We want to make telemetry data interoperable, which means we would now want to discuss a little more about like schema and evolution.

217
00:21:33,590 --> 00:21:35,630
How is schema evolution across layers being handled?

218
00:21:35,630 --> 00:21:45,111
When the ingest layer changes, for example, how do you make the downstream layers adapt if you're using different types of query languages here? That's a great question.

219
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:58,991
And that's one of the things that I actually find most interesting about the difference between the business intelligence data and very explicitly logs. Logs don't have a schema.

220
00:21:58,991 --> 00:22:01,551
They do, but they don't. There's repetition.

221
00:22:01,560 --> 00:22:04,230
There's common repetition in logs.

222
00:22:04,230 --> 00:22:08,430
They say the same thing over and over on different log lines.

223
00:22:08,430 --> 00:22:12,550
And there's information embedded in logs that you can think of as fields.

224
00:22:12,550 --> 00:22:16,110
You could think of as columns and you want to extract them and do things with that.

225
00:22:16,110 --> 00:22:18,190
That's a very common thing in logs.

226
00:22:18,190 --> 00:22:21,430
But there's not really fundamentally a schema.

227
00:22:21,430 --> 00:22:27,750
It's just whatever the developer wrote out as their logs, that's the log line. And you're getting at stuff.

228
00:22:27,750 --> 00:22:33,750
And you see this with log-oriented query languages and log-oriented experiences.

229
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:47,910
Really, all of them have some way of allowing you to interact with the log itself doing term searches where you're just looking for strings inside of the log. You don't have field names. You don't know column names.

230
00:22:47,910 --> 00:22:56,350
You're just saying, "Give me all of the logs that have this word or this subset of letters, this wildcard search type of a thing."

231
00:22:56,350 --> 00:23:03,590
They all have some experience that allows you to interact with the data by just finding letters or finding words in it.

232
00:23:03,590 --> 00:23:11,630
But then, they also have this notion of, "Well, when the log line looks like this, I want to extract these things from it.

233
00:23:11,630 --> 00:23:15,750
And I want to think about those things from an analytical perspective."

234
00:23:15,750 --> 00:23:21,070
And so, as an example, you might have a log line where someone's saying,

235
00:23:21,070 --> 00:23:30,350
"I just dealt with an HTTP request and it took me 20 seconds or 20 milliseconds or whatever and I returned 500 bytes."

236
00:23:30,350 --> 00:23:36,630
That plain text, that text might be in the log line, but you might want to pull out the 20 seconds or the 20 milliseconds as a latency measure.

237
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:40,750
You might want to pull out the number of bytes as a byte measure.

238
00:23:40,750 --> 00:23:44,910
And now, something like that, people tend to have very normal structure around.

239
00:23:44,910 --> 00:23:57,070
But a normal log line, there's a lot of times that we will put variables into the log line and it's nice to extract them out and think of them as their own column almost when interacting.

240
00:23:57,070 --> 00:24:04,150
And so, while it's both schema-less, people do they have assumptions of schema on the data,

241
00:24:04,150 --> 00:24:06,550
but all of the experiences,

242
00:24:06,550 --> 00:24:11,110
all of the query languages actually have this as a common thread where

243
00:24:11,110 --> 00:24:20,790
anything that's built around dealing with logs has some way to just find the line based on the characters and

244
00:24:20,790 --> 00:24:26,390
another way to interact with these fields that have been pulled out of it in order to gain more value.

245
00:24:26,390 --> 00:24:35,310
And so, I think the shift really is that the thing to unify on is not can we figure out a unified schema.

246
00:24:35,320 --> 00:24:47,990
The thing to unify on is can we find the commonalities in the actual language and the actual way that people are querying and support all of the different query languages.

247
00:24:47,990 --> 00:24:56,030
And when you shift to that, there's actually a lot more commonality in how the query languages need to operate than there is in the actual shape of the data.

248
00:24:56,040 --> 00:25:02,830
To make telemetry actually portable, we would need some form of standardization, don't you think?

249
00:25:02,830 --> 00:25:08,430
Like how would you standardize the event or the log or the metric or the trace schemas?

250
00:25:08,430 --> 00:25:11,230
Or do you not do that at all?

251
00:25:11,230 --> 00:25:15,630
So, in my mind, the standardization comes from the query language.

252
00:25:15,630 --> 00:25:19,950
So, my belief is that standards are almost always de facto standards.

253
00:25:19,950 --> 00:25:24,150
The only standards that actually ever exist are de facto standards because people adopted them.

254
00:25:24,160 --> 00:25:27,669
And then maybe the thing that everyone adopted,

255
00:25:27,669 --> 00:25:37,630
a standards body got built around and they evolved it and fixed stuff because a de facto standard always comes with some organic evolutionary element to it because

256
00:25:37,630 --> 00:25:45,510
someone just randomly dreamed it up because it's what they needed and then everyone started using it and then the cracks started being seen.

257
00:25:45,510 --> 00:25:48,190
But anyway, so a de facto standard takes shape.

258
00:25:48,190 --> 00:25:51,510
Everyone starts using it. A standards body gets built around that and then it goes.

259
00:25:51,510 --> 00:25:53,910
And that's really where standards come from.

260
00:25:53,910 --> 00:26:01,750
And I think the thing with the actual structure and shape of data is that each individual team,

261
00:26:01,750 --> 00:26:08,150
each new application, each new thing is always necessarily going to change the shape of the data.

262
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:15,950
But the thing that doesn't necessarily change is the language that people want to use to interact with the data.

263
00:26:15,950 --> 00:26:25,950
And so, the real standard is not trying to figure out how that data should be shaped, but figuring out how that data should be interfaced with.

264
00:26:25,950 --> 00:26:27,510
And that's really the query language.

265
00:26:27,510 --> 00:26:29,910
And so, to me,

266
00:26:29,910 --> 00:26:39,470
that's the actual key to the standardization so that you can decouple is supporting the query languages because that's the thing like even as all of these walled gardens,

267
00:26:39,470 --> 00:26:44,150
all of the vertically integrated stacks, they have some sort of query language.

268
00:26:44,150 --> 00:26:52,070
They have some sort of query API between their data layer and their visualization layer.

269
00:26:52,070 --> 00:26:59,230
And the simple hand-wavy thing, if you can support all of those APIs, you can support all of those interaction layers.

270
00:26:59,230 --> 00:27:01,910
And each of those APIs is fundamentally a query language.

271
00:27:01,920 --> 00:27:15,750
If you support all of those APIs, how would you maintain things like context like trace log correlation because that kind of stuff is important for debugging.

272
00:27:15,750 --> 00:27:20,431
Would that be a problem when data is flowing through these multiple systems? That's the thing here.

273
00:27:20,431 --> 00:27:25,070
The key is not that the data's flowing through multiple systems.

274
00:27:25,080 --> 00:27:38,311
When you implement that query language, when you implement that query API, all of the data's residing in one place and you have multiple different query APIs on it. And so, you can think of traces.

275
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:46,590
Traces, you have spans which are individual interactions and then they combine together via trace ID to make a whole chunk of interactions, right?

276
00:27:46,590 --> 00:27:50,950
You can think of each of those spans as a log.

277
00:27:50,950 --> 00:27:56,030
And then you can think of a trace as the collection of all of the logs that happened together, right?

278
00:27:56,030 --> 00:28:02,950
Now, if you have those logs in the same system that is implementing your trace queries,

279
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:09,790
you can now from a log-oriented query language, you can interact with all of those spans as logs.

280
00:28:09,790 --> 00:28:17,750
From a trace-oriented query language, you can interact with that group of logs as if it was a trace.

281
00:28:17,750 --> 00:28:25,149
And so, from that one place, by having the data in one place and implementing different query languages on it,

282
00:28:25,149 --> 00:28:34,430
each of those query languages can basically put their opinion, their assumptions of the data on top of that data and allow you to look at it from that lens.

283
00:28:34,430 --> 00:28:40,230
And so, the underlying data itself is not actually changing.

284
00:28:40,230 --> 00:28:44,470
It's the lens that the query language puts on top of it that gives you a different view.

285
00:28:44,480 --> 00:28:51,110
Let's think of these observability tools and these languages, PromQL, LogQL, SQL.

286
00:28:51,120 --> 00:29:01,191
Can you walk me through how a trace ID would flow from application code through collection, storage, and into a dashboard? Sure.

287
00:29:01,191 --> 00:29:05,909
So, OpenTelemetry actually does define a general schema for spans and traces and how that is.

288
00:29:05,919 --> 00:29:10,790
And all of the spans will have a trace ID field as JSON objects.

289
00:29:10,790 --> 00:29:16,149
But so, they'll have a trace ID field and that would be the same field that runs through all of them.

290
00:29:16,149 --> 00:29:18,470
So, each of your systems might generate a span.

291
00:29:18,470 --> 00:29:26,950
That would all flow through an OpenTelemetry collector or something like that, which would then route it into your observability, your data layer.

292
00:29:26,950 --> 00:29:32,990
So, you would get multiple different spans from multiple different systems all routed into the data layer.

293
00:29:32,990 --> 00:29:36,710
Now, each of those spans, they're JSON objects in the OpenTelemetry world.

294
00:29:36,710 --> 00:29:38,870
So, each of those spans is a JSON object.

295
00:29:38,870 --> 00:29:40,870
You can think of a JSON object as a log line.

296
00:29:40,870 --> 00:29:44,550
You can think of it as just some random text that's a log line.

297
00:29:44,550 --> 00:29:57,310
And so, you might have that in your data system and you could use your SPL, you could use your elastic query, you could use your log QL to interact with each individual span as if it was a log line.

298
00:29:57,310 --> 00:30:06,190
And so, if there's a message on that span about what happened in the system, you can see that as a log, you can interact with it, you can find it,

299
00:30:06,190 --> 00:30:11,310
and anybody or any dashboard that is basically showing activities that happened in logs,

300
00:30:11,310 --> 00:30:18,470
they would see the spans in response to those queries and it would show up dashboards there.

301
00:30:18,470 --> 00:30:21,990
Now, on that exact same data, there's a trace ID running through the whole thing.

302
00:30:21,990 --> 00:30:28,950
So, from the log experience, you could put the trace ID into your filter and you could see all of the logs that happened with that trace.

303
00:30:28,950 --> 00:30:35,870
But, if you now switch to a trace-oriented experience, it might already know that all of those log lines are combined and so,

304
00:30:35,870 --> 00:30:44,590
it's now asking you a question, okay, for all of the traces, what's the duration from the earliest to the latest?

305
00:30:44,590 --> 00:30:52,550
And how many of them happened and and and what occurred and how many errors happened or or things like that where in that trace experience,

306
00:30:52,550 --> 00:30:57,630
usually it's taking multiple of those logs, pulling out information from a couple of them,

307
00:30:57,630 --> 00:31:08,150
maybe doing subtraction of timestamps between them to find duration or something, pulling those out and then doing analysis over the things that it pulled out.

308
00:31:08,150 --> 00:31:11,910
So, that data can flow through open telemetry, can flow through whatever.

309
00:31:11,910 --> 00:31:15,350
It doesn't even have to all flow through the same way that you get data.

310
00:31:15,350 --> 00:31:21,630
You could have some come from logstash, some come from open telemetry, some come from a Splunk forwarder.

311
00:31:21,630 --> 00:31:30,190
As long as it has that same trace ID, you can use that to correlate the items when you're trying to look at things from the lens of a trace.

312
00:31:30,200 --> 00:31:38,550
You can also use that to find the individual items when you're trying to look at the lens through the lens of a log.

313
00:31:38,550 --> 00:31:40,830
And you can also from those things have metrics.

314
00:31:40,830 --> 00:31:42,630
You can pull metrics out of that.

315
00:31:42,630 --> 00:31:50,910
You can generate metrics and aggregate them up and flow those through kind of metric-oriented system as well.

316
00:31:52,950 --> 00:32:03,870
In an industry where demand for verified skill has never been higher, prove you have the foundation to deliver [music] results with the IEEE Computer Society's new software professional certification for emerging professionals.

317
00:32:03,880 --> 00:32:06,830
Give your career the head start it deserves.

318
00:32:06,830 --> 00:32:14,230
Visit computer.org/certification today.

319
00:32:16,550 --> 00:32:26,669
Do you see some sort of universal observability schema that comes in here where the industry would standardize everything or is that a little too wishy?

320
00:32:26,669 --> 00:32:35,070
I fundamentally do not believe there will ever be a universal schema to data because I see the shape of data similar to like us as human beings where

321
00:32:35,080 --> 00:32:42,111
each new human that's born is just a little bit different from everybody else. And yes, there's commonalities.

322
00:32:42,120 --> 00:32:47,030
Yes, there's things that look similar, but it all ends up being different.

323
00:32:47,030 --> 00:32:52,590
So, I don't believe we will ever have one universal schema.

324
00:32:52,590 --> 00:32:56,910
I do believe that there will be generally agreed upon commonalities.

325
00:32:56,920 --> 00:33:06,350
And people will run with it, but just the reality on the ground is that

326
00:33:06,350 --> 00:33:14,950
all it takes is one developer to use a field named X for some new reason and now your schema's all destroyed.

327
00:33:14,950 --> 00:33:18,430
It no longer means what it used to mean.

328
00:33:18,430 --> 00:33:21,750
And that just happens so often and so regularly.

329
00:33:21,750 --> 00:33:26,070
I think believing that that can be achieved is a fool's errand.

330
00:33:26,070 --> 00:33:32,669
It just can't be achieved, so you should just assume it can't be achieved and then figure out how to deal with it.

331
00:33:32,669 --> 00:33:38,471
I want to pivot into talking about query compute and the separation in practice. Yeah.

332
00:33:38,471 --> 00:33:47,270
So, when storage is decoupled from the query engine and we don't care about what type of query is happening, do indexing strategies change here?

333
00:33:47,280 --> 00:33:55,190
The interesting thing is the indexing strategies really align with the query languages you're looking to support.

334
00:33:55,190 --> 00:33:57,030
And fundamentally, that's the difference.

335
00:33:57,040 --> 00:33:59,710
The raw data doesn't change.

336
00:33:59,710 --> 00:34:09,349
You can think of of an indexing strategy as a lens that you've put on top of the data to make it easier to jump into certain parts of that data.

337
00:34:09,349 --> 00:34:13,710
It's a way that you're viewing the data to optimize things.

338
00:34:13,710 --> 00:34:25,510
And so, like, if you want to actually do full text search of data as a log line, you need to have some sort of index that allows you to do full text search.

339
00:34:25,520 --> 00:34:27,149
You can also just brute force it.

340
00:34:27,149 --> 00:34:32,710
You can just say, whatever, I'm just going to brute force it and find things, but there's a performance penalty for that.

341
00:34:32,720 --> 00:34:38,790
If you always have to look through all of the data in order to brute force a search,

342
00:34:38,790 --> 00:34:48,869
eventually, you're going to pay more in CPU than you would if you indexed the data and had the structures that allowed you to just jump right to the right data.

343
00:34:48,869 --> 00:34:53,869
And so, the indexing structures really align with the query pattern.

344
00:34:53,879 --> 00:35:03,150
And they align with the way in which you want to interact with the data more than aligning with the actual data itself.

345
00:35:03,160 --> 00:35:04,870
Does that make sense?

346
00:35:04,870 --> 00:35:06,870
It does make sense.

347
00:35:06,870 --> 00:35:08,910
Speaking of performance penalties, you mentioned this.

348
00:35:08,910 --> 00:35:11,590
Does decoupling add any kind of overhead?

349
00:35:11,600 --> 00:35:18,590
What are the latency versus throughput tradeoffs for incident response workflows, for example, when milliseconds matter?

350
00:35:18,600 --> 00:35:24,430
So, decoupling storage and compute absolutely can add latency.

351
00:35:24,430 --> 00:35:34,190
And fundamentally, there is a cold start problem where if you don't have the data to look at yet, the query has to get the data.

352
00:35:34,190 --> 00:35:40,990
The system the the physical machine that is running the query has to get the data to actually look at it.

353
00:35:40,990 --> 00:35:43,510
And so, there is a cold start latency problem.

354
00:35:43,510 --> 00:35:50,750
You mentioned kind of detections or what we've seen is there's two general use cases that people have over this data.

355
00:35:50,750 --> 00:35:55,030
And we separate them into detections and investigation.

356
00:35:55,030 --> 00:35:57,470
Now, detections are always on, always running.

357
00:35:57,470 --> 00:36:01,790
They're running on things just trying to see is something bad happening?

358
00:36:01,790 --> 00:36:03,590
Is something going wrong?

359
00:36:03,590 --> 00:36:06,110
Is Is there anything going on?

360
00:36:06,110 --> 00:36:08,310
This tends to happen on the most recent data.

361
00:36:08,310 --> 00:36:17,190
There's a very quick drop-off where people are doing detections on the most recent 5 minutes, the most recent 15 minutes, the most recent hour.

362
00:36:17,190 --> 00:36:21,550
Once you get 3 days old, 7 days old, you're not really doing active detections on that data anymore.

363
00:36:21,550 --> 00:36:28,510
You just have it there just in case or when you want to come and do a longitudinal analysis,

364
00:36:28,510 --> 00:36:40,510
when you want to look quarter to date or the bigger thing is sometimes those detections, and this definitely happens in the security world, those detections alert you to an intruder in your network.

365
00:36:40,510 --> 00:36:45,349
They alert you to something going on that's wrong and now you have to figure out when it started.

366
00:36:45,359 --> 00:36:55,631
And so now, maybe you know about this IP address that is suspicious and you're like, uh-oh, this stuff bad was happening from this IP address. What happened?

367
00:36:55,631 --> 00:37:03,270
And now you need to go back and you need to look and find all the things that happened from that IP address.

368
00:37:03,270 --> 00:37:05,270
And maybe in finding that, you find new IP addresses.

369
00:37:05,270 --> 00:37:08,830
And now you're like, okay, I need to expand the search to include this one now.

370
00:37:08,830 --> 00:37:10,710
You find new things and you're going back.

371
00:37:10,710 --> 00:37:14,270
And the real thing about this is you don't know how far back you're going to need to go.

372
00:37:14,280 --> 00:37:21,790
You just kind of need to go back until you're like, well, I've gone back far enough and I'm not finding anything anymore, so it must be done, right?

373
00:37:21,790 --> 00:37:26,470
And you don't know what breadth of data you need to look at.

374
00:37:26,470 --> 00:37:28,550
You don't know which systems they eventually got into.

375
00:37:28,550 --> 00:37:30,110
You don't know where they got.

376
00:37:30,110 --> 00:37:31,630
You don't know what they did.

377
00:37:31,630 --> 00:37:37,950
And so, you're really investigating the full set of your data and everything that you're needing to run with.

378
00:37:37,950 --> 00:37:45,110
And this separation of use cases, in the detection case, you absolutely do not want the cold start problem.

379
00:37:45,110 --> 00:37:51,270
You want things to be always on, you want them to be always running, but you're also always querying and interacting with the data.

380
00:37:51,270 --> 00:38:02,070
And so, it makes sense to be spending CPU cycles on that and have infrastructure that's always running and doing things because it is actually always running and you're always doing stuff with it.

381
00:38:02,070 --> 00:38:06,710
But, for the investigations, you're only going to look at that data when you're doing the investigation.

382
00:38:06,710 --> 00:38:09,070
You're only going to look at that data at certain times.

383
00:38:09,070 --> 00:38:13,710
And so, there's a notion of kind of ephemeral compute or ephemeral storage.

384
00:38:13,710 --> 00:38:25,950
And the public clouds really introduced this quite widely, this notion of being able to do things serverless, being able to spin up CPUs just to do a thing and then spin them down.

385
00:38:25,950 --> 00:38:37,349
And getting that into the data world, getting that into the security investigation world, I think it's an important element and aspect of things that decoupling storage and compute really enables.

386
00:38:37,349 --> 00:38:46,750
And in that world, yes, there's a cold start problem, but that's fine because storing the data, having the data accessible was what you were trying to achieve.

387
00:38:46,750 --> 00:38:55,710
Having that in S3 kept it cheap it kept it economical, having it in cloud storage and object store, whatever, kept it economical.

388
00:38:55,710 --> 00:38:57,631
But, now you actually need it. Now you actually need to query it.

389
00:38:57,640 --> 00:39:02,231
And while you're querying it, you want to do things fast. You want to do things rapidly. You're going to iterate.

390
00:39:02,231 --> 00:39:04,190
It's not just one query and then you're done.

391
00:39:04,200 --> 00:39:09,950
You're going to do a query, then you're going to do another query, then you're going to do another query, and you're going to keep iterating.

392
00:39:09,950 --> 00:39:20,230
And so, you really want a high degree of flexibility there where the cold start is not as big of a deal, but you need to maintain performance and speed across queries.

393
00:39:20,240 --> 00:39:22,590
That's really where the rub comes in.

394
00:39:22,600 --> 00:39:25,270
What about caching strategies?

395
00:39:25,270 --> 00:39:29,030
How do they work in this decoupled architecture?

396
00:39:29,030 --> 00:39:31,110
Where does caching actually happen?

397
00:39:31,110 --> 00:39:40,030
The most common approach, and I mean, well, it just physically has to happen this way, but the actual physical machine that is running the CPU cycles for the query,

398
00:39:40,040 --> 00:39:50,550
that's actually executing the query, has to have some amount of the data cached because CPUs they're interacting with memory on the physical machine.

399
00:39:50,550 --> 00:39:55,750
That memory is going to be populated either by reading disk or reading something off the network.

400
00:39:55,750 --> 00:40:04,590
When you're going to be iteratively querying things, like if all you ever need to do is query it once and then you're done, you might as well just read it off the network and throw it away.

401
00:40:04,590 --> 00:40:07,110
There's no point in actually caching stuff.

402
00:40:07,110 --> 00:40:13,190
But as I mentioned earlier, you're going to query it once, you'll find an IP address that you care about.

403
00:40:13,190 --> 00:40:15,030
Now you're going to want to query for that IP address.

404
00:40:15,030 --> 00:40:18,311
It's the same data, but you're now looking for a new IP address. You're looking for a new thing.

405
00:40:18,320 --> 00:40:31,270
At that point you're doing multiple iterative queries on the exact same data and there you want the data on disk so that it can then be loaded into memory from disk rather than having to go back out to the network, right?

406
00:40:31,270 --> 00:40:42,430
And so a very common strategy is you have some physical machines that when they get the query, they have the data, they make it available for query, they're querying it.

407
00:40:42,430 --> 00:40:46,550
Then those machines go away and public cloud makes this incredibly easy to do.

408
00:40:46,550 --> 00:40:50,710
You can just spin up some EC2 instances, fill up their disks, throw them away, move on with your life.

409
00:40:50,710 --> 00:40:56,110
And so that's the most common high-level idea of caching.

410
00:40:56,110 --> 00:41:02,150
It's infinitely deep though because the thing you actually need to respond to a query,

411
00:41:02,160 --> 00:41:11,030
like maybe there's 100 megabytes of data and you only need to look at 1 megabyte in order to actually respond to that query.

412
00:41:11,040 --> 00:41:21,190
And so you can get into a a really deep amount of depth of trying to be like, well, I don't want to download the whole 100 megabytes just to look at 1 megabyte.

413
00:41:21,190 --> 00:41:30,990
Is there any way I can figure out that I only need this 1 megabyte and only download that so that I can speed up the cold start and then I keep that cash and if I need another megabyte, fine, I'll grab that when I need it.

414
00:41:31,000 --> 00:41:38,910
That's kind of the the fundamental push and pull of what it means to cash in these decoupled architectures.

415
00:41:38,910 --> 00:41:41,910
Where does Apache Druid come in?

416
00:41:41,910 --> 00:41:43,990
What role does it play in this architecture?

417
00:41:43,990 --> 00:41:48,471
And can you also pivot into talking a little more about where Imply comes in? Yeah.

418
00:41:48,480 --> 00:41:53,030
Yeah, so talking a bit about Imply and Apache Druid.

419
00:41:53,030 --> 00:41:55,550
So real brief introduction.

420
00:41:55,550 --> 00:42:00,510
Imply is a company we started as a commercial entity around the open source Apache Druid project.

421
00:42:00,520 --> 00:42:08,710
Apache Druid's been doing distributed OLAP oriented analytical processing queries for a while.

422
00:42:08,710 --> 00:42:17,870
We've recently introduced this product called Lumi, which is a log oriented experience built on top of Apache Druid.

423
00:42:17,870 --> 00:42:23,230
And so it is Druid at the core with other things around it.

424
00:42:23,230 --> 00:42:30,390
It's its own product and what it's doing is it's leveraging Druid and leveraging the internals of Druid, which can do this kind of on the fly caching.

425
00:42:30,400 --> 00:42:37,350
It can it can do distributed it it handles queries across a distributed cluster.

426
00:42:37,350 --> 00:42:44,590
It handles availability, uptime, a whole bunch of operational concerns that make it easy to just kind of scale out and deal with stuff.

427
00:42:44,590 --> 00:42:49,350
We're we're running in Apache Druid, but we've added our own smarts.

428
00:42:49,350 --> 00:42:51,990
We've added our own compression technology.

429
00:42:51,990 --> 00:42:54,550
We've added log specific compression.

430
00:42:54,550 --> 00:42:59,230
We've added log specific indexing on top of the log specific compression.

431
00:42:59,230 --> 00:43:08,830
And we bundled all of that up into Druid and we've implemented multiple query languages on top of that same data so that you can pull that

432
00:43:08,830 --> 00:43:13,830
data in and you can now interact with it for multiple different ecosystems kind of all at the same time,

433
00:43:13,840 --> 00:43:21,630
which is is really what I was talking about earlier where this is fundamentally decoupling the interaction layer.

434
00:43:21,630 --> 00:43:31,030
It's it's decoupling the Splunk UI, the Kibana, the Grafana from the actual data layer itself and allowing a user,

435
00:43:31,030 --> 00:43:37,550
allowing an admin to put data into us and give that to whichever UI people want to work with it.

436
00:43:37,550 --> 00:43:40,430
Even Tableau, even Looker, even the SQL based ones.

437
00:43:40,440 --> 00:43:44,950
I want to talk about the operation of these pipelines end to end.

438
00:43:44,950 --> 00:43:55,990
What collection and routing patterns work best like OpenTelemetry collectors and vendor agents and gateways do they fit into this pipeline?

439
00:43:56,000 --> 00:44:08,790
Yes, in terms of our product, we support OpenTelemetry forwarders, Splunk forwarders, a heck format, and a number of things like that.

440
00:44:08,790 --> 00:44:20,670
One of the things we've seen people ask for the most with this though is we've seen a tendency in this world where people are routing their data into cloud object store.

441
00:44:20,670 --> 00:44:22,390
They've already got the data flowing into S3.

442
00:44:22,390 --> 00:44:27,110
They've already got it flowing into Google Cloud Storage.

443
00:44:27,110 --> 00:44:29,590
They've already got it flowing into Azure Blob Storage.

444
00:44:29,590 --> 00:44:35,390
They already have the data flowing into some cloud object store because it's cheap.

445
00:44:35,390 --> 00:44:37,590
It's a known quantity.

446
00:44:37,590 --> 00:44:40,910
Their organization works with it. They know how to get there.

447
00:44:40,910 --> 00:44:44,190
There's a whole bunch of tooling for just dumping data into those places.

448
00:44:44,200 --> 00:44:49,271
And then they're like, well, I have it here. Let me query that, please.

449
00:44:49,271 --> 00:44:55,270
And so that's one of the interesting things that I'm not quite sure exactly how it's all going to play out.

450
00:44:55,270 --> 00:44:57,390
So OpenTelemetry of course works.

451
00:44:57,390 --> 00:44:59,630
Your forwarders work, all of that.

452
00:44:59,630 --> 00:45:08,870
But a lot of times people are just dumping the stuff into a cloud object store and then saying I'm going to figure out how to query it after the fact.

453
00:45:08,870 --> 00:45:13,870
And so that's also a very common pipeline that honestly I think it makes a ton of sense.

454
00:45:13,870 --> 00:45:20,590
It's like, oh yeah, I totally get that because you know, if it's in that object store and you can interact with it, then you can take it anywhere.

455
00:45:20,590 --> 00:45:24,670
It kind of breaks the vendor lock-in to a certain degree, but we can take it a step further.

456
00:45:24,670 --> 00:45:34,070
If we can make that cloud data queryable by all of your different interaction patterns, by all of the different interaction layers, now you can put it in the cloud,

457
00:45:34,070 --> 00:45:39,430
make it available and have all your users interact with it. That's the vision of where we're going.

458
00:45:39,430 --> 00:45:45,270
That's what that's what we're trying to enable is that the unit economics,

459
00:45:45,270 --> 00:45:56,230
the cheap unit economics of I'm just going to store it in the cloud combined with making that data available with whatever interaction tool people actually want to use.

460
00:45:56,240 --> 00:46:01,911
Let's talk about this decoupled architecture again. Mhm.

461
00:46:01,911 --> 00:46:10,311
When you think of sampling for example, which is critical decision, right, to determine how to decide what you want and what you want to drop. Yep.

462
00:46:10,311 --> 00:46:16,950
How do you avoid losing high value signals like errors and slow requests in a decoupled architecture?

463
00:46:16,950 --> 00:46:27,349
There's a common challenge with sampling and really this comes down to anytime you choose to throw data away, you are making a choice today that you will never need that data.

464
00:46:27,359 --> 00:46:34,270
And if that's true, if it's true that you really never need that data, it was a great choice.

465
00:46:34,270 --> 00:46:38,070
It's the most economical thing you can do with that data if you never need it.

466
00:46:38,080 --> 00:46:41,630
It's absolutely the best.

467
00:46:41,630 --> 00:46:45,830
The problem is what happens when it turns out you did need that data.

468
00:46:45,830 --> 00:46:54,190
What happens when it when it turns out and so like one of the things that happens especially with like tracing is a user says they had an experience.

469
00:46:54,190 --> 00:46:56,990
Developers go and look at the traces and they're like, no, that didn't happen.

470
00:46:56,990 --> 00:46:58,950
It doesn't show up in any of the traces.

471
00:46:58,950 --> 00:47:01,590
And so that user's experience is a micro experience.

472
00:47:01,590 --> 00:47:04,510
It's one experience out of millions.

473
00:47:04,520 --> 00:47:08,030
Maybe it gets caught in the sample, maybe it doesn't.

474
00:47:08,030 --> 00:47:16,830
You have this happen often enough and you have an end user experience of this thing doesn't work and you have developers saying, no, it's always working.

475
00:47:16,830 --> 00:47:19,510
What are you talking about?

476
00:47:19,510 --> 00:47:21,110
And eventually like you you can't actually figure out the system.

477
00:47:21,110 --> 00:47:25,150
The other thing is you can sample all you want,

478
00:47:25,150 --> 00:47:38,190
but I think one of the thing that's really happening in the industry right now is with AI and with everything that's going on, there's a lot more government interest and regulatory interest in what should happen with data.

479
00:47:38,200 --> 00:47:45,830
Where data should be, how long it should be kept for and what should happen with it.

480
00:47:45,830 --> 00:47:53,071
And as soon as it's a data set that the government has regulated you must keep for XYZ period of time, you can't sample it away. You can't get rid of it.

481
00:47:53,080 --> 00:47:59,990
And so really though sampling fundamentally comes down to a question of how do I improve the unit economics?

482
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:05,390
How do I improve the cost bottom line of what I'm dealing with?

483
00:48:05,390 --> 00:48:11,190
It's really an economic question more than it is an actually meaningful technological question.

484
00:48:11,190 --> 00:48:15,750
It's just an easy technological answer to the cost problem.

485
00:48:15,750 --> 00:48:27,630
There's other routes like compression that you can also follow that help to reduce unit cost allowing you to store more without actually throwing it away where you can actually gain access to it.

486
00:48:27,630 --> 00:48:32,390
And so to me I see sampling on one side and compression on the other of a spectrum.

487
00:48:32,400 --> 00:48:40,470
And absolutely, no matter how good your compression is, if you 100% know you do not need that data, throw it away, please.

488
00:48:40,480 --> 00:48:42,230
Great advice.

489
00:48:42,230 --> 00:48:46,750
What about the observability of the observability pipeline itself?

490
00:48:46,750 --> 00:48:53,991
How do you make sure that your telemetry system is Observability of the observability pipeline. So as as a great question.

491
00:48:54,000 --> 00:48:59,990
This always comes down to, you know, it's turtles all the way down is is a type of a thing.

492
00:48:59,990 --> 00:49:03,550
And so you you have the observability watching the observability watching the observability.

493
00:49:03,550 --> 00:49:13,510
And so I mean, I can talk about for us what we do is we have an internal system that collects all of the data generated by our production systems.

494
00:49:13,510 --> 00:49:15,630
And so we we use our products to observe our product.

495
00:49:15,640 --> 00:49:19,310
Then the question is where does that product point its stuff to?

496
00:49:19,310 --> 00:49:24,310
And at some point you have something that's pointing its stuff to itself.

497
00:49:24,310 --> 00:49:27,590
For an end user, for an end customer,

498
00:49:27,590 --> 00:49:35,270
hopefully you can work with some vendor who's helping you provide observability and then they're the ones who are doing the recursive thing where

499
00:49:35,270 --> 00:49:44,671
they're providing the observability back into themself and you get a production grade product that you can work with, and you don't have to worry about like that. That's the hope.

500
00:49:44,680 --> 00:49:55,510
But, if you're doing things homegrown, if you're if you're getting to that, at some point, you're going to have a system that you're generating stuff, and you should put it into the same system that you have.

501
00:49:55,520 --> 00:50:03,670
But, that recursion, you're going to have to come down to a point where there's one system that's putting its own observability stuff into itself.

502
00:50:03,680 --> 00:50:10,990
Let's say I have a team that decided to decouple the stack the way that you recommended.

503
00:50:11,000 --> 00:50:21,990
What are some common failure modes that this team could have [clears throat] that would showcase that we didn't really decouple correctly, or we did a poor job of it?

504
00:50:22,000 --> 00:50:29,670
Failure modes for why the decoupling didn't really work.

505
00:50:29,670 --> 00:50:34,270
I guess I'm going to switch it to talk about what what does it mean to be successful.

506
00:50:34,280 --> 00:50:38,831
And if you didn't achieve success, then it's a failure mode. Let's call it that.

507
00:50:38,840 --> 00:50:42,590
And so, what does it mean to be successful?

508
00:50:42,590 --> 00:50:51,590
And really, in my mind, success is you were able to put the data in one place, and you don't have to move that data,

509
00:50:51,600 --> 00:50:59,870
but multiple different teams can now interact with that data, and they did not have to learn a new tool.

510
00:50:59,880 --> 00:51:10,590
Whatever tool they have been historically using, they're continuing to use that, and they now have access to more data. If that happened, it's been successful.

511
00:51:10,600 --> 00:51:21,430
If that hasn't happened, if putting the data in that place now requires each of these teams to learn a new tool, to build a new workflow, to change what they're doing, it's been unsuccessful.

512
00:51:21,440 --> 00:51:31,110
So, would a decoupled architecture actually cost more, or would it like allow for easier control because it's not costing as much? What is the cost delta you're putting in?

513
00:51:31,120 --> 00:51:38,790
be more cost effective because it should allow for reducing duplication of storage.

514
00:51:38,790 --> 00:51:40,950
It should allow for more effective compression.

515
00:51:40,950 --> 00:51:49,550
It should allow for more effective utilization of CPU resources, of storage resources, of all of that sort of stuff.

516
00:51:49,550 --> 00:51:52,990
And so, it fundamentally should be more cost effective.

517
00:51:53,000 --> 00:52:01,830
What kind of governance problems could potentially emerge if multiple query tools are querying the same data lake?

518
00:52:01,830 --> 00:52:04,110
Would there be like access control issues?

519
00:52:04,110 --> 00:52:09,270
You absolutely need to have access control issues, or access control implemented.

520
00:52:09,280 --> 00:52:10,830
You need to be able to control that.

521
00:52:10,840 --> 00:52:23,910
Another element of it is sometimes you want different teams to share the same data, but you don't want them to share the same physical CPUs.

522
00:52:23,910 --> 00:52:34,070
Because sometimes your business analysts, their query patterns are going to look very different from your observability team, from your security team.

523
00:52:34,080 --> 00:52:45,270
And you can run into noisy neighbor type situations where some query that looks crazy to an observability or security person,

524
00:52:45,270 --> 00:52:54,670
but makes tons of sense to a business analyst, hogs all of the CPU resources, and doesn't allow the security or or observability practitioner to do their job.

525
00:52:54,670 --> 00:53:03,470
And so, it's much more common to want to have the controls both around access control, but also around infrastructure control,

526
00:53:03,480 --> 00:53:15,470
to be able to allow for different chunks of infrastructure and compute and CPU to be used for different use cases,

527
00:53:15,470 --> 00:53:21,910
so that you can avoid people kind of stepping on each other's toes in terms of getting their job done.

528
00:53:21,910 --> 00:53:30,790
Let's say we have a team that is very stubborn, and we force them to work in a decoupled architecture, and they still want to use multiple tools with governance.

529
00:53:30,790 --> 00:53:36,870
They still want to use Grafana alongside of everything that is already implemented.

530
00:53:36,870 --> 00:53:39,270
Can they do it safely and with governance?

531
00:53:39,270 --> 00:53:42,790
If the decoupling is done right, yes.

532
00:53:42,790 --> 00:53:44,550
That's kind of the definition of success.

533
00:53:44,550 --> 00:53:53,670
If that team really wants to use Grafana, they should be able to use Grafana, and another team should be able to use Splunk,

534
00:53:53,670 --> 00:53:58,510
and another team should be able to use Kibana without any issues between them.

535
00:53:58,510 --> 00:54:04,870
They should all be able to see the same data, and continue doing the workflow that they want.

536
00:54:04,870 --> 00:54:13,590
And so, that that's that's really the key of the decoupling, and especially decoupling at the query language, is that that stubborn team shouldn't have to change their workflow.

537
00:54:13,600 --> 00:54:17,550
That stubborn team should get to keep their workflow.

538
00:54:17,550 --> 00:54:19,310
They just need more access to data.

539
00:54:19,320 --> 00:54:21,430
That suffices.

540
00:54:21,430 --> 00:54:27,830
I'll talk about the tradeoffs, and maybe when is it not a good idea to decouple?

541
00:54:27,830 --> 00:54:32,110
At what scale does it not make sense to do this? What would you say?

542
00:54:32,120 --> 00:54:38,110
The only time when it could maybe not make sense is actually at a small scale.

543
00:54:38,120 --> 00:54:51,030
At the very very beginning, when you're just like, I'm just setting some stuff up, and I'm going to get some things in, and I just want to be able to see stuff, maybe at that point, like you just want to pick something and run with it.

544
00:54:51,040 --> 00:54:52,830
Simplicity in that case would be helpful.

545
00:54:52,840 --> 00:55:03,950
Right. And so, if anything, it would be small scale that would cause you to go with the verticalized walled vendor, rather than any sort of large scale.

546
00:55:03,950 --> 00:55:09,590
The problems of kind of silos and enterprises and

547
00:55:09,590 --> 00:55:19,910
big organizations and different stubborn people wanting to use different UIs, those become much more painful the larger scale you get, rather than the smaller scale.

548
00:55:19,910 --> 00:55:25,790
When you're a team of three people, you can usually work through that stubborn person who really wants to use that one UI.

549
00:55:25,790 --> 00:55:35,990
For team that is locked in with a specific vendor the way that we talked about before, what does a realistic migration path look like?

550
00:55:35,990 --> 00:55:39,390
How do we talk about the timeline, the team size, what they learn?

551
00:55:39,400 --> 00:55:53,030
If it's done right with what I defined as success earlier, the migration path basically becomes, generally speaking, there'll be some data set that isn't available in that walled garden.

552
00:55:53,030 --> 00:55:55,150
That would be valuable to have in in that garden.

553
00:55:55,150 --> 00:56:02,470
And so, the migration starts with, well, we can take this data set, we'll put it in the decoupled world,

554
00:56:02,470 --> 00:56:08,790
and if it then becomes available in the walled garden, great.

555
00:56:08,790 --> 00:56:12,630
We've proven that we can add data to this walled garden by putting it in this decoupled thing.

556
00:56:12,630 --> 00:56:21,831
And you can go the route of, okay, let's start dumping more and more things in, because if you've actually put it in there, and you haven't impacted the end user workflow, what what's to stop you? Why not?

557
00:56:21,831 --> 00:56:30,910
It just becomes a migration of, okay, the data's here, the data's there, but it's still available via the same workflow, so it's easy.

558
00:56:30,910 --> 00:56:39,750
And so, I'd say it starts with if you've got that data set that you really want to make available, but you're not doing it due to cost restrictions,

559
00:56:39,750 --> 00:56:43,630
due to infrastructure, due to what whatever, you start with that.

560
00:56:43,630 --> 00:56:54,030
You proved yourself that you can actually add something here and not impact your end user's workflow, and then from there, it just becomes a question of what data you want to focus on.

561
00:56:54,030 --> 00:57:03,270
Is it more important for you to gain more access to more data sets that you didn't have before, or is it more important to take data sets that had been locked in a system and unlock them?

562
00:57:03,280 --> 00:57:07,470
And that can be done on a case-by-case basis, really.

563
00:57:07,470 --> 00:57:15,270
And so, I'd say that fundamentally, it can go as fast or as slow as someone wants and what makes sense for their organization.

564
00:57:15,280 --> 00:57:17,190
So, that's a decision left to the team.

565
00:57:17,200 --> 00:57:20,430
Mhm.

566
00:57:20,430 --> 00:57:30,752
What about for a team like this, would they need to have a specific skill set to operate in a decoupled observability stack that they might not have today? Maybe. It depends.

567
00:57:30,752 --> 00:57:41,311
As a vendor in the space providing a product in the space, all you have to do is give us money, and then you don't have to have the skill set because we'll just operate it for you. And then none of that's required.

568
00:57:41,320 --> 00:57:47,349
If you're trying to go it alone, do it yourself, then there might be some stuff.

569
00:57:47,349 --> 00:57:55,270
You Understanding data systems, understanding architecture, understanding distributed systems and stuff like that can become a bit of a thing,

570
00:57:55,270 --> 00:58:05,670
but I mean, those are also all skills that with the introduction of microservices and the separation of everything, I think people are starting to become a little bit more and more familiar with.

571
00:58:05,670 --> 00:58:08,430
And so, I don't know. I'd say yes and no. Mhm.

572
00:58:08,440 --> 00:58:10,310
I'm going to wrap up here.

573
00:58:10,310 --> 00:58:11,950
I want to ask you a couple more questions.

574
00:58:11,950 --> 00:58:20,110
If you could give one piece of advice to engineers evaluating whether or not to decouple their observability stack, what would it be?

575
00:58:20,110 --> 00:58:21,660
The one piece of advice is yes, it should be decoupled.

576
00:58:21,670 --> 00:58:22,910
[laughter]

577
00:58:22,920 --> 00:58:36,510
It'd be think about what do you think could become possible if that data set that you're dealing with, if that data set, if like you could suddenly get a new data set and and make it available to more teams.

578
00:58:36,520 --> 00:58:38,349
Like what could become possible?

579
00:58:38,359 --> 00:58:42,030
Thinking about that, I think, is an interesting thought exercise.

580
00:58:42,030 --> 00:58:46,511
Think about the future and what is ahead of you. It absolutely has to be decoupled.

581
00:58:46,520 --> 00:58:51,070
That's just naturally where things are going to go.

582
00:58:51,070 --> 00:58:52,910
So, Eric, well, thank you so much for your time today.

583
00:58:52,910 --> 00:58:59,230
Before we wrap up, would you like to direct our listeners on where to find you and more about your work and your company?

584
00:58:59,240 --> 00:59:02,631
I'm part of Imply. Website imply.io.

585
00:59:02,631 --> 00:59:08,710
Our new product Lumi, as in like illuminate, l u m i.

586
00:59:08,710 --> 00:59:12,150
Go to imply.io, read about Lumi, look us up, try us out.

587
00:59:12,150 --> 00:59:16,390
Yeah, I'd say that's the best way to connect and see everything.

588
00:59:16,390 --> 00:59:20,430
